+# Local Multica SDK shadow acceptance
+
+This opt-in suite exercises the actual local Multica server, daemon, Codex backend, persisted receipts, and browser. It does not start services. The default test suite skips every real-agent case before authentication or CLI access. The evidence helper and report tests are offline.
+
+The daemon backend switch is `MULTICA_CODEX_BACKEND=agent-adaptor`; an unset switch selects native Codex. The test's `MULTICA_SHADOW_BACKEND` labels the expected phase and does not change the daemon. Actual SDK startup/receipt/cleanup logs must prove the shadow phase used the SDK. Use separate daemon phases and fresh issues for the native/shadow comparison.
+
+## Preconditions and configuration
+
+Run Playwright in the same Ubuntu host/network namespace as the daemon: it reads the daemon's actual task files, and a real foreground provider shell calls the test's loopback HTTP gate. Codex must be able to reach that localhost gate. A browser, Python 3, the explicitly selected Codex CLI, and the managed environment must already be ready.
+
+Use this checkout's `make env-exec ARGS="pr30-sdk-shadow -- ..."` or the environment owner's managed wrapper. Do not copy another checkout's `.env`, use a personal workspace, or print credential values. `TestApiClient` supplies the existing E2E login/onboarding fixture; task completion and supplement delivery always use the real server/daemon path. There are no task/receipt DB updates in this suite.
+
+| Variable | Required value |
+| --- | --- |
+| `MULTICA_RUN_AGENT_ADAPTOR_SHADOW` | `1`, explicitly authorizing real model execution |
+| `MULTICA_SHADOW_BACKEND` | `native` or `agent-adaptor`, matching the running daemon |
+| `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` | Actual managed localhost API URL |
+| `PLAYWRIGHT_BASE_URL` | Actual managed localhost web URL |
+| `DATABASE_URL` | Actual managed localhost database URL, used by `TestApiClient` auth/onboarding |
+| `MULTICA_SHADOW_EMAIL` | Isolated `@localhost` test account; prepared account: `pr30-shadow@localhost` |
+| `MULTICA_SHADOW_WORKSPACE_SLUG` | Exact isolated workspace slug; prepared slug: `pr30-shadow` |
+| `MULTICA_SHADOW_RUNTIME_ID` | Actual online Codex runtime registered by this phase's daemon |
+| `MULTICA_SHADOW_MODEL` | Explicit model, for example `gpt-6.1-sol` |
+| `MULTICA_SHADOW_EFFORT` | Explicit effort, for example `xhigh` |
+| `MULTICA_SHADOW_DAEMON_LOG` | Absolute path to the running daemon's append-only slog file |
+| `MULTICA_SHADOW_REPORT` | Absolute JSON report path, separate for each phase/run |
+| `MULTICA_SHADOW_RUN_ID` | Unique operator-selected run identifier; never reuse a report for another run |
+| `MULTICA_SHADOW_PROJECT_ID` | Optional isolated project ID when a prepared project resource is required |
+| `MULTICA_SHADOW_TIMEOUT_MS` | Optional per-step timeout; default `480000` |
+| `MULTICA_SHADOW_CLEANUP` | Optional `1` deletes created issues/chats/agents through APIs; default retains them for UI inspection |
+
+The suite rejects workspace fallback to any other slug. The prepared API/web ports are currently `19014` and `13934`; confirm them against the managed environment rather than assuming they are available. Runtime ID and daemon log must come from the actual launched daemon. Authentication stays in the managed profile and browser memory; reports redact the acquired token.
+
+## Execution
+
+First run only the minimal comparison against the native daemon, with `MULTICA_SHADOW_BACKEND=native` and a new report/run ID. Then have the environment operator stop that daemon, start its SDK shadow counterpart with `MULTICA_CODEX_BACKEND=agent-adaptor`, and supply its actual runtime ID/log. Set `MULTICA_SHADOW_BACKEND=agent-adaptor` and another new report/run ID. Run the same command in each phase:
+
+```sh
+pnpm exec playwright test e2e/agent-adaptor-shadow.spec.ts --workers=1 --retries=0 --reporter=line
+```
+
+The native phase executes one development task. The shadow phase executes eight provider tasks across four cases, including two deliberately cancelled tasks; one withdrawn chat task never starts. Retries are disabled. A failure remains a failure even if later cases succeed. Compare the first case in both phase reports: identical development prompt and independent host acceptance, actual source/test files and hashes, tool output, task result/usage/timing, and browser screenshot. This is a small functional comparison, not a performance benchmark.
+
+The development gate runs fixed host Python commands against the actual task workspace. It first restricts `shadow_math.py` to the requested narrow pure-function fixture, then checks positive, negative, zero, and large-integer additions and runs the generated unittest suite with at least two discovered tests. The generated unittest must really succeed. Every command receipt binds task ID, source hashes before/after, argv, working directory, exit status, stdout/stderr, and log hash. Failed commands remain in the report. An echoed `SHADOW_TEST_OK` or a failed unittest followed by a successful shell command cannot satisfy this gate. The AST check is a narrow fixture constraint, not a general Python sandbox.
+
+Safe offline checks do not set the opt-in flag:
+
+```sh
+pnpm exec playwright test e2e/agent-adaptor-shadow.evidence.spec.ts e2e/agent-adaptor-shadow.report.spec.ts e2e/agent-adaptor-shadow.acceptance.spec.ts --workers=1 --reporter=line
+pnpm exec playwright test e2e/agent-adaptor-shadow.spec.ts --list
+pnpm exec playwright test e2e/agent-adaptor-shadow.spec.ts --workers=1 --reporter=line
+```
+
+The last command must report four skips. Discovery and offline helper tests do not log in, start a browser, resolve a user CLI, or call a model.
+
+## Assertions and evidence
+
+| Case | Actual path and required evidence |
+| --- | --- |
+| Development comparison | API structured explicit `mention://agent/<id>` → addressed actual runtime → source and unittest files → independent host arithmetic and generated-unittest receipts → completed task visible in browser. Shadow requires actual SDK RunID in daemon slog; native rejects SDK logs for that task. |
+| Current comment and next run | Foreground shell reaches host gate before browser input. Browser selects **Add to current run**; original member identity/comment ID, one exact delivered supplement receipt, matching SDK delivery log, and visible **Read by** are required. Retrying the strict supplement endpoint with the same client request ID must return the same historical comment. A foreign issue cannot retarget the task. An unknown explicit target cannot create an extra task. Browser **Start after this run** creates a distinct queued task; actual SDK cleanup of the old run precedes startup of the new SDK RunID. |
+| Stop and start over | Shell gate proves the old provider ran. Browser action must retain its cancelled task, partial tool log, and `stopped.txt`, then create a different completed task with `restarted.txt=NEW_GOAL`. SDK Done/cleanup must precede the new startup. |
+| Chat | Actual API creates the initial and queued messages; browser removes a queued message and selects **Steer** for another. The active task is cancelled, priority runs before ordinary FIFO input, the withdrawn task never starts, and a separate actual chat session remains empty. SDK cleanup/start ordering and real assistant task IDs prove the sequence. |
+
+Each case writes `actual-shadow-evidence.json` and screenshots in Playwright's output directory. The aggregate report contains all task API results/messages, final comments and receipts, selected actual artifact contents/hashes, relevant SDK slog lines, configuration IDs, timestamps, and assertion outcomes. Failure paths collect available partial task/messages before requesting cancellation of any remaining active test task. A cancellation request is recorded as a request; it is not proof of provider cleanup.
+
+The aggregate report requires one native case or all four shadow cases before `complete=true`; `passed=true` also requires every case to pass. It survives Playwright worker replacement, rejects duplicate cases or another run's report, and never converts partial evidence to success. Preserve both phase reports, Playwright output, and the original daemon logs. Do not claim UI acceptance from API records alone: the real browser cases and screenshots must pass.
+
+This suite validates the selected structured mention and ordinary-reply routes, not Multica's entire mention parser. Chat Steer is the product's cancel-and-prioritize flow, not SDK `Interrupt`; **Start after this run** is a new execution after Done, not SDK `AfterTurn`. It does not prove legacy provider-session import, durable daemon restart recovery, all plugins, or deployment behavior.
+
+The mapped behavior comes from the official Multica checkout at `0d627e54e1bd4d2521359f293d04521246495ce2`: `apps/docs/content/docs/mentioning-agents.mdx` (explicit targets, plain replies, four delivery choices), `apps/docs/content/docs/chat.mdx` (FIFO/Steer), and the actual comment/chat handlers and UI in this checkout.
